<div class="article-body">

<p>If you want the short answer on how much light lettuce needs indoors: <strong>14 hours a day at 200 to 300 PPFD</strong>, which lands you at a daily light integral of 10 to 15. That is it. Not 18 hours. Not 24. Not "as bright as you can get it." Lettuce is the one crop where turning the dial up makes it worse, and I have the bitter, brown-tipped trays to prove it.</p>

<p>Lettuce is a leaf factory, not a fruit factory. It does not need the sun you would give a tomato. It needs a soft, steady drizzle of light for exactly long enough, then a real night. Give it more and it stops being food and starts defending itself.</p>

<p>I figured this out the expensive way. I started with a cheap bar light on 16 hours and got decent lettuce. Then I "upgraded" to a stronger panel and pushed the timer to 20 hours, because every grow guide I read said more light equals more growth. My next three trays went leathery, bitter, and bolted at day 34. Let me save you the three trays.</p>

<h2 id="answer">1. The Short Answer: What to Set Today</h2>

<p>Three numbers, and they all have to work together. Here is what lettuce actually wants at the canopy, measured at the top of the plant:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> 14 hours on, 10 off. The research standard for commercial lettuce is 14 to 16, and 16 is where bolt-prone varieties start to tip over into flowering.</li>
  <li><strong>Intensity:</strong> 200 to 300 PPFD at the canopy is the comfortable home range on a 14-hour timer. Seedlings want the low end, 100 to 200.</li>
  <li><strong>The total:</strong> a DLI of 10 to 15 mol/m²/day. This is the number that combines the first two, and it is the only one your plant feels.</li>
</ul>

<p>Everything else is bookkeeping. A 250 PPFD bar for 14 hours gives 12.6, and a 300 PPFD panel for 12 hours gives 13.0, so two very different fixtures land in the same place. My shelf runs the cheaper, weaker version.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math: What "More Hours" Costs</div>
  <p>My 60W panel on 14 hours a day draws 0.84 kWh, about $3 a month at 12 cents a unit. Push the same panel to 20 hours and you spend about $4.30. You are not saving money by running it longer either way. The extra 6 hours cost me $1.30 a month and the whole crop.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="mistake">2. Why "More Light" Wrecked My Tray</h2>

<p>I thought I had it figured out. The first run was 16 hours under a single 40W strip and it worked. Green leaves, no drama, salad every ten days. So when a friend told me a 100W panel would double my harvest, I bought it and hung it 12 inches over the tray. Then I set the timer to 18 hours, because if 16 was good, 18 had to be better.</p>

<p>Three days in, the leaves on the outer edge curled. I read that as "not enough light," which is the exact wrong read, so I left it on 20 hours the following week.</p>

<p>Then the tips went brown and crispy. Not dry, not wilting, just dead tissue on the youngest inner leaves. The head stayed low and tight, like the plant was cringing. By day 34 the center shot up a stem and my lettuce had turned into a sad flower stalk. Whole tray in the bin.</p>

<p><strong>This is where it gets interesting.</strong> Every guide I had read gave me hours, and every guide gave me intensity, and none of them told me what happens when you raise both at once. I did the math after the fact. A 100W panel at 12 inches puts out roughly 300 PPFD at that height. Run that for 20 hours and the DLI is 21.6. That is four points above the ceiling where lettuce starts to burn. I had not given it more light. I had given it heat stress with a light bill.</p>

<h2 id="dli">3. The Only Number That Matters: DLI</h2>

<p>Wattage is on the box and hours are on the timer, so those are the two numbers beginners obsess over. Neither one is what the plant measures. The plant measures the total light that arrived, all day, added up. That is the daily light integral, or DLI.</p>

<p>The formula is simple enough to do on a phone:</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The DLI Formula</div>
  <p><strong>DLI = PPFD × hours × 0.0036</strong></p>
  <p>Example: 200 PPFD for 14 hours is 200 × 14 × 0.0036 = 10.1, the bottom of the range. Bump the same light to 250 PPFD and you get 12.6, comfortably inside. Same formula, whatever light you own.</p>
</div>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the widely cited floor for lettuce comes from Erik Runkle at Michigan State, who set a minimum DLI of 12 to 14 for commercial production under long days. The greenhouse tables push the optimum to 14 to 17. But run those numbers on a 14-hour timer and they collide with the plant. A DLI of 14 needs 278 PPFD, and 17 needs 337, which is past the 300 PPFD point where tipburn starts. So the honest home band is 10 to 15, and the practical ceiling is about 15. Butterhead lands at the top of it, looseleaf in the middle, red-leaf varieties need the most light to color up properly.</p>

<p>Below about 150 PPFD, lettuce goes leggy: long pale stems, thin translucent leaves, a plant stretching toward an exit. It is the same look as a seed tray left too far from the light. Between 200 and 300 PPFD, lettuce grows compact and dense. Above 300 PPFD the leaves get thick and leathery, the margins curl, and tipburn arrives on schedule. That last jump is the one that caught me.</p>

