<h2 id="hours">4. The Trap in the Hours Table</h2>

<p>Here is where the internet splits into two camps that are both right and both unhelpful.</p>

<p>Camp one says "run your lettuce 18 to 20 hours, light is free growth." Camp two says "lettuce is a long-day plant, anything past 14 hours triggers bolting." If you read only one and stop, you pick a number based on half the story.</p>

<p>Lettuce is a facultative long-day plant. Past about 14 to 16 hours, early-flowering varieties push up a flower stalk, and heat makes it worse. But bolt-resistant cultivars can take 20 hours, even continuous light, and put on 6 to 23 percent more mass with no visible damage. So the hours question has no single answer. It depends on your variety and your room temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Here is why that matters:</strong> the two camps are describing two different failures on the same dial. Camp one is optimizing total light and forgetting the day length. Camp two is protecting against bolting and forgetting the intensity. My mistake was obeying camp one with a strong light. If I had run that 100W panel for 20 hours I hit 21.6 DLI and tipburned. If I had run my original weak strip for 20 hours I would have hit maybe 9 DLI and grown spaghetti. Same hours, opposite disasters.</p>

<p>The honest answer is that hours are the wrong knob to turn first. Set hours at 14, because that is safe for every variety, and then raise or lower intensity to land in the DLI range. You get one knob instead of two, and the safe one is the one that cannot trigger flowering.</p>

<h2 id="measure">5. How to Hit the Range Without a $400 Meter</h2>

<p>You do not need a laboratory PAR meter. You need a rough number and a way to read the plant. Two tools cover it.</p>

<p>The first is a cheap <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=lux+meter+for+grow+lights&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital lux meter</a>, about $12. It reads lux, not PPFD, but you can convert: for white and full-spectrum LEDs, roughly 1 PPFD is about 70 to 75 lux. So the 200 to 300 PPFD target is a lux reading of somewhere near 15,000 to 22,000 at the canopy. It is not exact physics. It is close enough to tell you whether you are at 8,000 lux, which is too dim, or 40,000, which is too hot.</p>

<p>The second tool is your eyes, on these three signals:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Long pale stems, thin leaves:</strong> below the range. Move the light closer or go to 16 hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Brown crispy inner leaf tips:</strong> above the range. This is tipburn, and it is a light and airflow problem, not a calcium problem.</li>
  <li><strong>Thick leathery leaves, tight head:</strong> past 300 PPFD. Raise the fixture or dim it.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last point is worth sitting with. Tipburn gets blamed on calcium, and everyone buys a calcium supplement that does nothing. The real cause is that calcium moves through the plant in the water stream, and the inner leaves sit in still, humid air where almost no water moves. The plant-factory research on this is consistent: a steady horizontal airflow across the canopy, even something as gentle as 0.3 meters per second, cuts tipburn sharply, while dead air under the light lets it spread. Airflow, not a bottle. If your tips burn, check the distance between your light and your leaves first, then put a fan on them.</p>

<p>If you want to compare fixtures by measured output instead of box wattage before you buy one, the <a href="/grow-lights">grow light database</a> has the real numbers for the popular models. The <a href="/posts/grow-light-distance-chart">grow light distance chart</a> covers the spacing half of the same problem.</p>

<h2 id="buy">6. The Setup I Run, and the One Flaw</h2>

<p>Three ways this goes, and I paid for the first two.</p>

<p><strong>The trap.</strong> The 100W "full spectrum" panel that pulled 38 watts from the wall and cooked the tray at 12 inches. Strong light plus long hours is the classic beginner combo, and it is how I got my first tipburn. Big box wattage, small real output, and no dimmer to fix the mistake.</p>

<p><strong>The overkill.</strong> A commercial grow light with a spectrum controller, a PAR meter, and a schedule app. Genuinely how vertical farms hit uniform heads. You are growing salad for two people on a shelf, and a $400 PAR meter to read a $30 light is a bad trade.</p>

<p><strong>The middle ground I run.</strong> A 40W <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Barrina+T5+LED+grow+light+bar+2ft&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Barrina T5 LED bar</a> about 6 inches above the canopy, a $12 lux meter for the one-time setup reading, and a mechanical <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mechanical+outlet+timer+plug&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">outlet timer</a> set to 14 hours.</p>

<p>That is 250 PPFD at 6 inches for 14 hours, which is 12.6 DLI. I nudge the light to 5 inches on the next run if the newest leaves look pale, which gets me closer to 300 PPFD and 15 DLI. The last three trays at that setting gave me three cuts each and no tipburn, about 188 grams per plant across the run.</p>

<p>One addition I made after the tipburn run: a small <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=oscillating+clip+fan+grow+tent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">oscillating clip fan</a> on the lowest setting, aimed across the canopy, not at it. It cost about $16 and it is the reason my inner leaves stopped going brown. If you take one thing from the airflow research, take that.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth about my top pick. The Barrina bar has no dimmer and no switch. You cannot turn it down. So the only two settings you have are distance and hours. To hit a lower DLI you have to raise a fixture that wants to sit close, and you lose the even coverage that made it good in the first place. If you run a warm room or a bolt-prone variety, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=dimmable+LED+grow+light+panel+for+seedlings&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">dimmable LED panel</a> gives you the third knob and is the better buy, even if it costs more up front. I run the bar because my room stays cool, and I pay for that simplicity by not having a dial.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> set every lettuce light to 14 hours and leave the hours alone. Then aim for 200 to 300 PPFD at the canopy, which is roughly 15,000 to 22,000 lux on a $12 meter. That is a DLI of 10 to 15, the only number the plant reads. Put a fan on the canopy so the inner leaves do not tipburn, and put the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mechanical+outlet+timer+plug&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">timer</a> between the wall and the strip so you never forget. A good LED bar starts around $25 and a timer is about $10, so the whole light fix is under $40. I spent three trays and two lights to learn that more is not more. Let my wallet take the hit instead of yours.</p>

